{"Hawaiian":{"id":"8f7d8d3a-2f26-4979-b268-7d4d6939e107","word":"Hawaiian","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Hawaiian language","tags":["masculine"],"word":"havaiano"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["language","language"]],"glosses":["The Hawaiian language."],"id":"f16da6e2-b6f0-449b-8300-6be5ee2b841d"}],"related":[{"word":"Wiktionary’s coverage of Hawaiian terms"}],"synonyms":[]},"Hawaii":{"id":"6a2b794b-99a4-4e52-8460-41664467d035","word":"Hawaii","trans":[{"lang":"Brazilian Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"state of the United States","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havaí"},{"lang":"European Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"state of the United States","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havai"},{"lang":"Brazilian Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"chain of islands","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havaí"},{"lang":"European Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"chain of islands","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havai"},{"lang":"Brazilian Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"largest of the Hawaiian Islands","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havaí"},{"lang":"European Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"largest of the Hawaiian Islands","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havai"},{"lang":"Brazilian Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"kingdom","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havaí"},{"lang":"European Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"kingdom","tags":["masculine"],"word":"Havai"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I've learned a lot by watching my Democratic colleague from Hawaii Senator Daniel Inouye. He’s a man of uncommon decency. I’ve never seen him demand political loyalty because of party or friendship, and I’ve never known him to go back on his word.","ref":"2007, Joe Biden, “Jill”, in Promises to Keep, New York: Random House, published 2008, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, page 111:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[60,66]],"id":"34fc38ed-016d-4de1-b5fa-a7333220b883"},{"text":"The state where most intermarriages took place was Hawaii, where more than four in 10 newlyweds (42.4%) were intermarried.","ref":"2012 February 16, Ashley Hayes, “Study: Interracial marriage, acceptance growing”, in CNN, archived from the original on 02 Mar 2017:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[51,57]],"id":"8296ac48-2331-4950-8a10-fbcb3d5eb697"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Hawaii, USA","en:Places in the United States","en:States of the United States"],"links":[["insular","insular"],["state","state"],["United States","United States#English"],["Honolulu","Honolulu#English"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"Aloha State"},{"word":"Hawaii State"},{"word":"State of Hawaii"}],"glosses":["An insular state of the United States, formerly a territory. Capital: Honolulu."],"id":"7f3733e3-e02d-4bb9-9ccb-b28cfa4bb410"},{"categories":["en:Islands"],"links":[["archipelago","archipelago"],["Pacific Ocean","Pacific Ocean#English"],["North America","North America"],["Oceania","Oceania#English"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"Hawaiian Islands"},{"word":"Islands of Hawaii"},{"word":"Sandwich Islands","tags":["obsolete"]}],"hyponyms":[{"word":"Maui"},{"word":"Oahu"},{"word":"Kauai"},{"word":"Molokai"},{"word":"Lanai"},{"word":"Niihau"},{"word":"Kahoolawe"}],"glosses":["An archipelago of the Pacific Ocean, between North America and Oceania."],"id":"8eaf2d3c-178e-4a4d-8710-378c0a7076ae"},{"categories":["en:Islands","en:Places in Hawaii, USA","en:Places in the United States"],"links":[["island","island"],["Hawaii","Hawaii#English"],["Pacific","Pacific#English"],["Hawaii","Hawaii#English"],["Hawaii","Hawaii#English"],["United States","United States#English"],["Hawaiian Islands","Hawaiian Islands"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"Big Island","tags":["informal"]},{"word":"Hawaii Island"},{"word":"Island of Hawaii"}],"raw_glosses":["(specifically) An island of Hawaii archipelago, Pacific Ocean, of Hawaii County, State of Hawaii, United States. The largest of the Hawaiian Islands."],"glosses":["An island of Hawaii archipelago, Pacific Ocean, of Hawaii County, State of Hawaii, United States. The largest of the Hawaiian Islands."],"tags":["specifically"],"id":"403caa99-1777-45fb-8aaa-818c6d63e793"},{"categories":["English terms with historical senses","en:Former polities"],"links":[["kingdom","kingdom"],["Hawaiian Islands","Hawaiian Islands#English"],["Pacific Ocean","Pacific Ocean#English"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"Kingdom of Hawaii"},{"word":"Hawaii Kingdom"},{"word":"Hawaiian Kingdom"}],"raw_glosses":["(historical) A former kingdom in the Hawaiian Islands, Pacific Ocean."],"glosses":["A former kingdom in the Hawaiian Islands, Pacific Ocean."],"tags":["historical"],"id":"fdb80f5d-0c19-44cd-8805-da7f736810b9"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"hawk":{"id":"4e8dc1d9-bc8b-4c40-b716-4d950abbd707","word":"hawk","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to attempt to cough up, to clear the throat","word":"pigarrear"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to attempt to cough up, to clear the throat","word":"expectorar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to attempt to cough up, to clear the throat","word":"escarrar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"to hawk a loogie","bold_text_offsets":[[3,7]],"type":"example","id":"884e35fd-2d72-492c-8fa0-0e0f28253d72"},{"text":"[I]s a trobled with the cough a the Lunges ſtill? does he hawke anights ſtill?","ref":"c. 1603 (date written), Iohn Marston, The Malcontent, London: […] V[alentine] S[immes] for William Aspley, […], published 1604, →OCLC, Act II, scene ii:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[58,63]],"id":"0d461358-f725-432b-8eae-88178e02f71c"},{"text":"He hawked up, with incredible straining, the interjection ah!","ref":"1751, [Tobias] Smollett, chapter 117, in The Adventures of Peregrine Pickle […], volume (please specify |volume=I to IV), London: Harrison and Co., […], →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[3,9]],"id":"09ba0f2e-a213-4950-b8f0-dd023a300355"},{"text":"He had a new tough manner of pulling down breath and hawking into the street.","ref":"1953 September 18, Saul Bellow, chapter III, in The Adventures of Augie March […], New York, N.Y.: The Viking Press, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[53,60]],"id":"08a1b253-6145-4700-96c8-5817a9cce94e"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with collocations","English terms with quotations","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["expectorate","expectorate"],["cough up","cough up"],["clearing one's throat","clear one's throat"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, intransitive) To expectorate, to cough up (something, such as mucus) from one's throat; to produce (something) by coughing or clearing one's throat."],"glosses":["To expectorate, to cough up (something, such as mucus) from one's throat; to produce (something) by coughing or clearing one's throat."],"tags":["intransitive","transitive"],"id":"c2638649-93eb-4c13-8db3-f7c870c400dc"},{"examples":[{"text":"Grandpa sat on the front porch, hawking and wheezing, as he packed his pipe with cheap tobacco.","bold_text_offsets":[[32,39]],"type":"example","id":"5922cec1-2eb1-40f1-8ba2-15c63f350b6e"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["cough up","cough up"],["throat","throat"],["cough","cough"],["heavily","heavily"],["uvula","uvula"],["frication","frication"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, intransitive) To try to cough up something from one's throat; to clear the throat loudly; to cough heavily, especially causing uvular frication."],"glosses":["To try to cough up something from one's throat; to clear the throat loudly; to cough heavily, especially causing uvular frication."],"tags":["intransitive","transitive"],"id":"0a16783f-4490-4d72-8c6d-5fc7a1b70019"}],"related":[{"word":"Hawkshaw"},{"word":"hawkshaw"},{"word":"Hawkubite"},{"word":"winkle-hawk"}],"synonyms":[]},"haw":{"id":"ef439033-4ec0-42f2-9e44-8fcfa8619fe8","word":"haw","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"an instruction for a horse or other animal to turn towards the driver","word":"ôa"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"an instruction for a horse or other animal to turn towards the driver","word":"ôô"}],"pos":"intj","tags":null,"senses":[{"coordinate_terms":[{"word":"gee"}],"glosses":["An instruction for a horse or other animal to turn towards the driver, typically left."],"id":"f8397320-ed06-4232-8fec-5209ca6e8089"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"hawser":{"id":"af55ee2b-312f-4b46-9156-22b547ba3430","word":"hawser","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"cable","tags":["feminine"],"word":"amarra"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Near-synonym: mooring line","id":"2b615c9f-ef68-47ea-bfae-51b7f31123a9"},{"text":"The hawser was as taut as a bowstring, and the current so strong she pulled upon her anchor. All around the hull, in the blackness, the rippling current bubbled and chattered like a little mountain stream.","ref":"1881–1882, Robert Louis Stevenson, “The Ebb-tide Runs”, in Treasure Island, London; Paris: Cassell & Company, published 14 November 1883, →OCLC, part V (My Sea Adventure), page 185:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,10]],"id":"ad94f335-6ce0-4ab3-8ea3-20aeaa695fec"},{"text":"A hatchet to my hawser? all adrift to go?","ref":"1888–1891, Herman Melville, “[Billy Budd, Foretopman.] Chapter [HTTP://GUTENBERG.NET.AU/EBOOKS06/0608511H.HTML CHAPTER 26].”, in Billy Budd and Other Stories, London: John Lehmann, published 1951, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[16,22]],"id":"786ae9d7-139d-4fb7-a373-6fae1de18e62"}],"topics":["nautical","transport"],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemm